off="50">0</td></tr></tbody></table></table-wrap>. The dose-limiting toxicity comprised myelosuppression and DLT was reached at 90&#x02009;mg&#x02009;m<sup>&#x02212;2</sup>. The incidence of grade 3 or worse neutropenia at below 35&#x02009;mg&#x02009;m<sup>&#x02212;2</sup> was zero out of three patients, at 35&#x02009;mg&#x02009;m<sup>&#x02212;2</sup> one out of seven patients, at 50&#x02009;mg&#x02009;m<sup>&#x02212;2</sup> two out of sixpatients, at 70&#x02009;mg&#x02009;m<sup>&#x02212;2</sup> four out of seven patients, at 90&#x02009;mg&#x02009;m<sup>&#x02212;2</sup> three out of three patients. Neutropenia was seen at dose levels of 35&#x02013;90&#x02009;mg&#x02009;m<sup>&#x02212;2</sup> in 17 (65&#x00025;) of patient with the nadir occurring between days 8 and 15 postinfusion and with recovery to baseline levels by day 22. Thrombocytopenia of grades 1&#x02013;3 was seen from dose levels 35&#x02013;90&#x02009;mg&#x02009;m<sup>&#x02212;2</sup> in 10 (38&#x00025;) patients. Anaemia grades 1&#x02013;3 was seen from dose levels 20&#x02013;90&#x02009;mg&#x02009;m<sup>&#x02212;2</sup> in 20 (77&#x00025;) patients. There was no grade 4 thrombocytopenia or anaemia at any dose level.</p><p>SP1049C induced nonhaematological toxicity at each dose level is shown in <xref rid="tbl3" ref-type="table">Table 3</xref><table-wrap id="tbl3" position="float"><label>Table 3</label><caption><p content-type="table-title">Nonhaematological toxicities (worst grade per patient)</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups" border="1" width="85%"><colgroup><col align="left"/><col align="char" char="."/><col align="char" char="."/><col align="char" char="."/><col align="char" char="."/><col align="center"/></colgroup><thead valign="bottom"><tr><th align="left" valign="top" charoff="50"><bold>Adverse event</bold></th><th align="char" valign="top" char="." charoff="50"><bold>Dose level (mg&#x02009;m<sup>&#x02212;2</sup>)</bold></th><th align="char" valign="top" char="." charoff="50"><bol